From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: nci: enumerate NFCEEs after init (secure element probe)

Card emulation on the S3NRN4V is done by its embedded secure element, not by
the host: an Android transit app provisions a MIFARE card into the eSE, the
applet lives in the eSE's own secure storage and survives an OS change, and the
eSE runs the MIFARE/Crypto1 exchange autonomously once the controller routes
the RF field to it. Reaching that from Linux starts with learning the eSE's
NFCEE id, which the controller reports in an NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF.

The generic NCI core already implements NFCEE_DISCOVER (the command, the
response and the notification handler that records ndev->hci_dev->nfcee_id),
but nothing ever issues the command -- the mainline flow only enumerates NFCEEs
from a driver's discover_se op, and s3fwrn5 has none. Issue NFCEE_DISCOVER once
at the end of nci_open_device, right after RF_DISCOVER_MAP, so the eSE is
enumerated on every adapter bring-up. It is best-effort: the NFCC answers with
num_nfcee = 0 if it has none, and a failure does not stop the adapter coming up.

The S3NRN4V is an NCI 2.0 controller, and the mainline NFCEE code speaks NCI
1.0 in two places that make an NCI 2.0 part reject the whole exchange:

  - NCI 2.0 dropped the Discovery Action parameter from NFCEE_DISCOVER_CMD, so
    the command carries no payload. Sending the 1.0 one-byte action to this
    controller is answered with SYNTAX_ERROR (0x05). Send an empty command on
    NCI 2.0.
  - NCI 2.0 shortened NFCEE_DISCOVER_RSP to a single status byte (the NFCEE
    count and details moved to per-NFCEE NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTFs). The mainline
    response handler only understood the 1.0 two-byte form and treated the
    reply as a protocol error. Accept the 2.0 form: on an OK status leave the
    request pending so a following NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF completes it.

With both fixes the controller answers NFCEE_DISCOVER with status OK and reports
its NFCEEs. On rhodep it enumerates two: an embedded secure element at NFCEE id
0x83 whose only supported protocol is 0x80 (MIFARE) -- this is where the transit
card lives -- and the UICC. This is the first step towards routing the MIFARE
listen to the eSE so the provisioned card answers a reader (the remaining work
is NFCEE_MODE_SET to enable 0x83 and a protocol route MIFARE -> 0x83 in the
listen mode routing table, plus RF_DISCOVER in listen).
--- a/net/nfc/nci/core.c	2026-09-01 04:01:08.370033003 +0000
+++ b/net/nfc/nci/core.c	2026-09-01 04:22:30.953203013 +0000
@@ -530,6 +530,15 @@
 				   msecs_to_jiffies(NCI_INIT_TIMEOUT));
 	}
 
+	/* Enumerate the NFCEEs once the controller is initialised. Card
+	 * emulation on this hardware is done by the embedded secure element
+	 * (which holds a provisioned MIFARE transit card), so the first step is
+	 * to learn the eSE's NFCEE id from the resulting NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF.
+	 * The NFCC returns num_nfcee = 0 if it has none. Best-effort: a failure
+	 * here must not stop the adapter from coming up. */
+	if (!rc)
+		nci_nfcee_discover(ndev, NCI_NFCEE_DISCOVERY_ACTION_ENABLE);
+
 	clear_bit(NCI_INIT, &ndev->flags);
 
 	if (!rc) {
@@ -671,6 +680,14 @@
 	struct nci_nfcee_discover_cmd cmd;
 	__u8 action = (unsigned long)opt;
 
+	/* NCI 2.0 dropped the Discovery Action parameter: NFCEE_DISCOVER_CMD
+	 * carries no payload. Sending the 1.0 one-byte action to an NCI 2.0
+	 * controller (the S3NRN4V) is rejected with SYNTAX_ERROR (0x05). */
+	if (ndev->nci_ver & NCI_VER_2_MASK) {
+		nci_send_cmd(ndev, NCI_OP_NFCEE_DISCOVER_CMD, 0, NULL);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	cmd.discovery_action = action;
 
 	nci_send_cmd(ndev, NCI_OP_NFCEE_DISCOVER_CMD, 1, &cmd);
--- a/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c	2026-09-01 04:08:59.403150013 +0000
+++ b/net/nfc/nci/rsp.c	2026-09-01 04:43:10.773343004 +0000
@@ -246,6 +246,21 @@
 {
 	const struct nci_nfcee_discover_rsp *discover_rsp;
 
+	/* NCI 2.0 shortened NFCEE_DISCOVER_RSP to a single status byte; the
+	 * number of NFCEEs is no longer carried here, each is reported by its
+	 * own NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF. The S3NRN4V is an NCI 2.0 controller and
+	 * answers with one byte, which the 1.0 parsing below (len != 2) would
+	 * wrongly reject as a protocol error. Accept it: on OK, leave the
+	 * request pending so a following NFCEE_DISCOVER_NTF completes it, and
+	 * on error complete now. */
+	if (ndev->nci_ver & NCI_VER_2_MASK) {
+		u8 status = skb->len ? skb->data[0] : NCI_STATUS_NFCEE_PROTOCOL_ERROR;
+
+		if (status != NCI_STATUS_OK)
+			nci_req_complete(ndev, status);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (skb->len != 2) {
 		nci_req_complete(ndev, NCI_STATUS_NFCEE_PROTOCOL_ERROR);
 		return;
